{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"findings\":[{\"title\":\"Bare file operands evade resolved-path restrictions\",\"file\":\"src/gnosis/kernel/policy.py\",\"line\":134,\"severity\":\"critical\",\"claim\":\"_looks_like_path treats a normal filename such as \\\"passwd\\\" or \\\"build\\\" as non-path, so it is omitted from resolved_paths even though command interpreters use it as a path.\",\"scenario\":\"With cwd=/etc, parse_command([\\\"rm\\\",\\\"passwd\\\"], cwd) produces no resolved path. The workspace rule demonstrated in tests iterates only resolved_paths and therefore returns ALLOW; the real action deletes /etc/passwd.\"},{\"title\":\"Flag-attached paths and quoted shell paths are normalized incorrectly\",\"file\":\"src/gnosis/kernel/policy.py\",\"line\":107,\"severity\":\"critical\",\"claim\":\"parse_command classifies every dash-prefixed token as a flag without examining attached values, and shlex.split(..., posix=False) retains POSIX quote characters in string commands.\",\"scenario\":\"tar --directory=/outside -xf archive has /outside only inside flags and thus no resolved target. Likewise a POSIX-shell command such as rm -rf \\\"/outside/file\\\" retains the quotes, becomes a path under cwd lexically, and can satisfy a /workspace prefix rule even though the shell removes quotes and targets /outside/file.\"},{\"title\":\"Approval resolution can combine a decision for one action with approval for another\",\"file\":\"src/gnosis/kernel/policy.py\",\"line\":363,\"severity\":\"critical\",\"claim\":\"resolve_escalation never verifies decision.action_id equals snapshot.action_id. It looks up approval using the supplied snapshot but returns ALLOW carrying the supplied decision's action_id.\",\"scenario\":\"Obtain an ESCALATE decision for action A, grant/obtain approval for different action B, then call resolve_escalation(decision_for_A, snapshot_B, store). It returns ALLOW. A caller executing B has an approval path without B ever having been evaluated by the policy rules.\"},{\"title\":\"A supposedly frozen approved action can be changed after authorization\",\"file\":\"src/gnosis/kernel/policy.py\",\"line\":155,\"severity\":\"critical\",\"claim\":\"ActionSnapshot is only shallowly frozen: payload and context are mutable dictionaries, including nested mutable values. PolicyDecision retains only a hash string and no immutable execution copy.\",\"scenario\":\"An adapter decides or resolves approval for a snapshot whose payload contains a safe command/request, then a caller mutates payload[\\\"command\\\"] or a nested request field before execution. The prior ALLOW and action_id remain in hand while the executed action differs from the evaluated action.\"},{\"title\":\"Malformed RuleOutcome values escape instead of failing closed\",\"file\":\"src/gnosis/kernel/policy.py\",\"line\":304,\"severity\":\"major\",\"claim\":\"Only the RuleOutcome container type is checked. verdict, reason, detail, and transform contents are unvalidated before reason.startswith and _PRECEDENCE indexing.\",\"scenario\":\"A rule returning RuleOutcome(\\\"ALLOW\\\", \\\"ok\\\") causes a KeyError at _PRECEDENCE; RuleOutcome(Verdict.ALLOW, None) causes AttributeError at startswith. decide raises rather than returning the required runtime_error DENY, inviting unsafe caller fallback behavior.\"},{\"title\":\"The enforcement matrix does not check enforcement against adapters or reality\",\"file\":\"src/gnosis/kernel/policy.py\",\"line\":405,\"severity\":\"major\",\"claim\":\"EnforcementMatrix is an unconstrained, caller-supplied label registry. Its tests only verify retrieval of labels entered in the same test; no adapter capability, implementation hook, required restriction set, or runtime evidence is checked.\",\"scenario\":\"An integration can declare EnforcementClaim(\\\"adapter\\\", \\\"network\\\", HARD) while the adapter has no network control. The matrix and its tests pass, and a consumer can treat the restriction as mechanically enforced despite no actual enforcement.\"}],\"overall\":\"FAIL: the parser has direct path-classification bypasses, escalation accepts mismatched decision and approval inputs, and snapshot mutability breaks the claimed exact-action binding. These can produce ALLOW for a different or outside-scope real action, so the unit is not safe to use as an action gate.\"}"}}
